<title>GERMANY: Waigel says can't see EMU without France.</title>
<headline>Waigel says can't see EMU without France.</headline>
<dateline>GEMERING, Germany 1997-07-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>German Finance Minister Theo Waigel said on Saturday that Europe's economic and monetary union (EMU) would really be inconceivable without France's participation.</p>
<p>Asked by journalists at a meeting of the Bavarian Christian Social Union (CSU) if EMU were conceivable without France, Waigel said: &quot;Only theoretically, not practically.&quot;</p>
<p>Waigel, who on Friday said he expected a German budget deficit this year of around 70 billion marks -- well up from previous forecasts of 53.3 billion, also said Germany will reach the EMU entry target for budget deficit to GDP of three percent.</p>
<p>&quot;We will make it,&quot; Waigel said. --Bonn newsroom, 49 228 260 97160</p>
</text>
